b/ref 1080 "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404952 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404952 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "311b8139-e8df-52d9-8b84-27e1171d5a1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404953 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404953 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404953 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404953 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404953 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0a65ec3-c24d-50c1-a1d2-f9666a246122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404953 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1,e)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,1,e)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =y\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(1,1,e)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =-\\frac{1}{z}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{z}(1,1,e)=-\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,1,e)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,1,e) & =⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,1,e)+∇f(1,1,e)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨1,1,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-1,z-e⟩ \\\\  & =0+1(x-1)+1(y-1)-\\frac{1}{e}(z-e) \\\\  & =-1+x+y-\\frac{z}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404954 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404