{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y+z}}, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y+z}}, & \\, & {f}_{z}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y+z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,2,1),$ estimate the value of $f(-0.01,1.98,1.05).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504665 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adb4921d-df66-5c35-baf2-cc613e0cb3d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504665 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,2,1)+∇f(0,2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-2,z-1⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨{f}_{x}(0,2,1),{f}_{y}(0,2,1),{f}_{z}(0,2,1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-2,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,2,1)$, ${f}_{y}(0,2,1)$ and ${f}_{z}(0,2,1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2,1) & =\\frac{2{e}^{0}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+2+1}}=1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2,1) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+2+1}}=1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(0,2,1) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+2+1}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈8+⟨1,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-2,z-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(-0.01,1.98,1.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-0.01,y=1.98$ and $z=1.05,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-0.01,1.98,1.05) & ≈8+⟨1,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.01,1.98-2,1.05-1⟩ \\\\  & =8+⟨1,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.01,-0.02,0.05⟩ \\\\  & =8-0.01-0.02+0.05 \\\\  & =8.02\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504693 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504693 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504693 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z-{y}^{2}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504693 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d66b6ff3-5c30-5d09-a1a7-0b4d573341bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504693 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,2)+∇f(1,0,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,0,2)=2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =2xz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(1,0,2)=4, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-2y\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(1,0,2)=0, \\\\ {f}_{z} & ={x}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(1,0,2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,0,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,0,2) & =⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,2),{f}_{y}(1,0,2),{f}_{z}(1,0,2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,2)+∇f(1,0,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-2⟩ \\\\  & ≈2+⟨4,0,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-2⟩ \\\\  & ≈2+4(x-1)+0(y)+(z-2) \\\\  & ≈-4+4x+z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504698 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504698 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504698 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504698 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,0)=2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=4x,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=6y.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0),$ estimate the value of $f(-1.01,0.01).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfe98e1c-3b93-5422-932b-6229fbfa5965" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504698 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,0)+∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),y-0⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(-1,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩ \\\\  & ≈2+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(-1,0)$ and ${f}_{y}(-1,0)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,0) & =4(-1)=-4 \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,0) & =6(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈2+⟨-4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(-1.01,0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1.01$ and $y=0.01,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.01,0.01) & ≈2+⟨-4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1.01+1,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =2+⟨-4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =2-4(-0.01)+0(0.01) \\\\  & =2+0.04+0 \\\\  & =2.04\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
